8-Apr-97 - v1.18 Mille Bornes by Dotoran of Frontiers BBS!

If Upgrading from v1.17 Mille Bornes:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Just replace all command files and you'll be set to go! No other changes
were made to the files, other than a bug fix and the addition of a log entry.

If Upgrading from v1.15 or v1.16 Mille Bornes:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Just replace all command files and you're set! Check below concerning a
new variable, "breakinF", which can be changed in the "CNetMilles" command
file. (Search for "BREAKINF" in your text editor)

If Upgrading from v1.14 Mille Bornes:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Replace all files in this archive as usual, then...

    This version had a problem with the new smart path coding. A variable
conflict actually, which could produce extraneous files in the user's mail
directories. Please enter the following dos commands from within a cli:

            list mail:users/#?/_millesC#? lformat %P%N

    If any files were listed, follow the above command with this one:

            delete mail:users/#?/_millesC#?

    All extraneous files will then be removed from your system. These files
are NOT used in the game, but were created incorrectly inside the user's
mail directories. If a file exists, a high score will have been lost in the
process, but that's all.

If Upgrading from v1.11 thru v1.14 Mille Bornes:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    The only changes made were to the actual COMMAND file. No changes were 
made to the user config files or to the SAVED GAME files. Simply copy the 
GAME file "CNetMilles" as well as the HISTORY and README files over the ones 
you are currently using to update your versions.

    Also check below, under Installing for the First time, for info on adding
a command to your BBSMENU file. Without this command, the new QUICKSTART mode
will not be available to you or your users!

If Upgrading from v1.10 Mille Bornes:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
NOTE: Please ReRead the section under installing for the first time where
~~~~  it talks about adding those MCI commands to your sys.welcome file, as
      there's also a fix to make to the {JL} command(if coming from v1.07).

   RUN the included Update program! A note to those who feel a bit of 
"animosity" whenever you have to DELETE something; The update program gets 
run HERE at LEAST twice before I give it to you. Also, ANYTHING that is 
deleted WILL be shown to you ON SCREEN, so you'll ALWAYS know what is being 
removed from your systems. :-)

   This version creates High Score files and because of this, I've decided to
store the game in it's own directory, so you'll notice this version resides
in it's own directory, "Milles/". Delete any older version MB files you have,
then copy the "Milles/" directory to a path on your system(like in DOORS:).
You'll need to modify the location in the appropriate AT/EL screens, and in
BBSMENU, so that CNet can find the game, but that should be the only other
thing you have to do.

   Now refer to the section below on Installing for the 1st time for some more
information pertaining to a variable to edit as well as including the SHOW
file in your welcome files...

If Installing for the First Time:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
   Copy the included "Milles/" directory somewhere in your system, preferably
within your DOORS: assignment.

   Add the "<path>Milles/CNetMilles" as an ARexx PFile/Door into your system,
where <path> is the location you chose to store the "Milles/" directory.

   Create a "MILles" command in BBSMENU(preferably Menu # 2) by adding the 
following line:

             MILles     `3-     |   {<path>Milles/CNetMilles}
             ~|~        ^           ^~~~|~~
          Uppercase   Access   CTRL-Q   `--> Wherever you stored Milles/.

   Near the top of the "CNetMilles" command file, notice the "amigascores"
variable. If left at the "1" setting, then any of the Amiga Opponents can get
either a High Hand or a High Game score, but if changed to a "0", then only
Human players are eligible to obtain high scores. 

   Right below the "amigascores" variable is a NEW variable, "breakinF", which
was included in the v1.17 release. If left at it's default setting of "1", the
BreakIn option will be active during standard Human vs. Amiga games, however
if you're running Mille Bornes on a multi-line system and feel that Mille
Bornes is really hogging your system resources, try setting this value to "0"
which will effectively SKIP the ability to BREAKIN during regular games. The
file should use roughly 40% less system resources than previous versions, as
it will no longer need to sit in a do-loop for extended periods of time. The
commands for reading/sending OLM's, ReDrawing the screen, and Quitting will
still be available, but only during the user's turn. The BreakIn mode will
still ALWAYS be available during Amiga vs. Amiga games, even if you opt to
"Sit-In" one of the seats after a game has progressed.

   Starting with v1.12, if you're playing a PARTNER game and your PARTNER is
an Amiga Player, if it should play the winning points for your team, you will
be eligable to obtain a high score. This wasn't so in earlier versions!

   If interested, you can place the following call somewhere in your logon
sequence. This could be within a Logon Activities Menu, or an entry in your
"systext:sys.welcome" file:

            {T15#3}{JL+1}{#0<path>Milles/CNetMilles.Show}{1}
            |    | |     |                               |
            `----|-`-----`---> Ctrl-Q characters <-------'
                 |
                 `--> The LOWEST Access Group that can PLAY Mille Bornes!

   If a user with an access LOWER than the one you specify logs on, they will
NOT be shown the Mille Bornes screen whatsoever. This access group should be
the SAME one used in your BBSMENU entry(if you created one). Gotta love MCI!

NOTE: The addition of the + (plus) in the {JL+1} command above. This will
~~~~  stop problems from occurring if you ALREADY HAVE a {1} in your welcome
      file(perhaps used in conjunction with a `Quick Logon' prompt, etc). 

   You should replace <path> above with the directory you chose to store the
included "Milles/" directory. The "CNetMilles.Show" file will attempt to
randomly select a High Score file(if one exists). If it tries 5 times, but
still cannot locate one(as may be the case if you're installing for the first
time), then it will display an EMPTY file. To the bottom of the display, the
user will be asked if he/she wishes to play the game(with a default answer of
NO).

  That's It! You're ready to play! The SAVED GAME files are stored inside the
user's mail directory, so that if the user is ever purged from the system, so
will these SAVED GAME files.

  The High Score files are stored inside the Milles/ directory. There are 2
files created for each TYPE of game. You'll see:

        CNetMilles.HiHand2    thru    CNetMilles.HiHand6

                              and:

        CNetMilles.HiGame2    thru    CNetMilles.HiGame6

  created over time. To reset any high score file, simply DELETE it's file.
You will NOT see a "CNetMilles.HiHand5" or "CNetMilles.HiGame5" created, as
there is NO 5-Player game available.

